+# Synaptics Tudor (Kensington VeriMark DT, 047d:00f2) protocol notes
+
+Not for upstream. Sources: the python prototype from the synaTudor
+reverse engineering effort (06cb:00be, older firmware), plaintext traces
+of the Windows driver 6.0.11.1132 talking to this exact sensor
+(firmware 10.1), and live experiments with the driver in this branch.
+Where the prototype and the traces disagree the traces won.
+
+Everything here is verified on hardware unless it sits under "Unknowns".
+
+## USB
+
+- Two interfaces. Interface 0 is a FIDO U2F hid device and stays with
+  usbhid. Interface 1 is vendor class, with one interrupt IN endpoint 0x83
+  (8 bytes, events, unused so far). The driver claims interface 1 only.
+- Full speed, EP0 max packet 8 bytes.
+- Everything goes over vendor control transfers to the device, wIndex 0.
+  There are no bulk endpoints in use (the 06cb:00be prototype used some).
+
+| bmRequestType | bRequest | wValue | data | meaning |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 0x40 out | 0x16 | len mod 8 | command | send a command |
+| 0xc0 in  | 0x17 | ignored | reply | read the reply of the last command |
+| 0x40 out | 0x19 | 1 | none | seen first thing in every open, purpose unknown, not needed |
+| 0xc0 in  | 0x1a | 0 | 1 byte | reads 0x01 after 0x19, purpose unknown, not needed |
+| 0xc0 in  | 0x14 | 0 | 2 bytes | TLS state, byte 0 is 1 inside a session, 0 outside |
+| 0x40 out | 0x1b | 0 | none | sent before the closing alert, "session end" in the vendor log |
+| 0x40 out | 0x15 | | | bootloader/DFT, never used |
+
+### wValue of request 0x16
+
+The data stage is padded with zeros to a multiple of 8 bytes and wValue is
+the real length mod 8 (0 when the length is a multiple of 8). The Windows
+driver values all fit: GET_VERSION 1 byte -> 1, IOTA_FIND 17 -> 1, PAIR
+401 -> 1, ClientHello 84 -> 4, second TLS flight 615 -> 7, a 31 byte
+record -> 7, a 38 byte record -> 6, a 50 byte record -> 2. Wrong values
+on a TLS record make the sensor answer with a fatal alert (20 bad record
+mac, or 47 for 0 and 8). For cleartext GET_VERSION the values 0, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 7
+were all accepted, so the command length is implied by the opcode there.
+Bit 3 is ignored (15 works like 7).
+
+wValue of request 0x17 does not matter. The Windows driver uses 0x8000
+with wLength 4096 for IOTA reads and 0 otherwise, replies were identical
+for 0, 1, 0x4000 and 0x8000. wLength is only a cap, short replies are
+fine (256 for a 66 byte reply and so on). The driver uses 4096 for
+in-session commands.
+
+### Reply format
+
+Cleartext commands and in-session commands: 2 byte little endian status,
+then payload. Seen as success: 0x0000 (0x0412 and 0x05cc come from the
+prototype). TLS_DATA (0x44) replies are raw TLS records with no status.
+
+Right after a previous session ended without a close_notify (process
+killed), the first command gets an encrypted TLS alert record
+(`15 03 03 00 1a ...`) from the old session. Asking again returns the real
+reply. The driver retries up to 3 times.
+
+## Commands used
+
+Opcodes as seen on the wire. Names come from the vendor driver's own log
+("CMD SEND: VCSFW_CMD_...").
+
+| opcode | name | args | reply |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0x01 | GET_VERSION | none | 38 bytes, see below |
+| 0x93 | PAIR | host cert (400) | status, host cert (400), sensor cert (400) |
+| 0x44 | TLS_DATA | 3 zero bytes, then TLS records | raw TLS records |
+| 0x9e | DB2_GET_DB_INFO | `01` | 38 bytes payload, see below |
+| 0x9f | DB2_GET_OBJECT_LIST | type u8, 3 zero bytes, parent id (16) | count u16, then ids (16 each) |
+
+Seen in the Windows traces but not sent by this driver (not needed to get
+a session): 0x8e IOTA_FIND (`8e <id u16> 02 00 + zeros`, ids 0x09 and
+0x1a, 26 and 78 byte replies), 0xaf (`af 01 00 00 00`, 10 byte reply,
+unknown), 0x19 GET_STARTINFO (68 byte reply), 0x82 FRAME_STATE_GET.
+
+### Opcode names for 0x9d..0xa4
+
+The task description had 0x9d GET_DB_INFO, 0x9e OBJECT_LIST, 0x9f
+OBJECT_INFO, 0xa0 OBJECT_DATA. The live vendor driver log disagrees: the
+command that goes out as 0x9e is logged as DB2_GET_DB_INFO and 0x9f as
+DB2_GET_OBJECT_LIST, the same as proto.txt in the prototype repo. So the
+numbering from proto.txt (0x9e info, 0x9f list, 0xa0 object info, 0xa1
+object data, 0xa3 delete or cleanup, 0xa5 format) looks right and the
+0x9d-0xa4 table is shifted by one. The driver only sends 0x9e and 0x9f
+and refuses everything else, see tudor_cmd_allowed(). Nothing from 0xa0
+upwards is ever sent, so the safety rules hold under either numbering.
+
+### GET_VERSION reply (38 bytes with status)
+
+Offsets with the status bytes included.
+
+| offset | size | field |
+|---|---|---|
+| 6 | 4 | firmware build (0x00315df5 here) |
+| 10 | 1 | firmware major (10) |
+| 11 | 1 | firmware minor (1) |
+| 13 | 1 | product id ('A') |
+| 18 | 6 | sensor id |
+| 24 | 1 | flags1, bit 0 advanced security |
+| 25 | 1 | flags2, bit 5 selects the "-kf" sensor key |
+| 37 | 1 | provision state (3 = provisioned) |
+
+### DB2_GET_DB_INFO reply (38 bytes of payload)
+
+u16 dummy, u16 version major, u16 version minor, u32 pversion, then u16:
+UOP_len, TOP_len, POP_len, template slot size, payload slot size, then
+three counters (current, deleted, available) each for users, templates,
+payloads. Field names are from the vendor driver's verbose log, offsets
+matched by hand against the log values.
+
+This sensor: v1.0, pversion 4, users 3 (124 free), templates 2 (9 free),
+payloads 4 (+2 deleted, 19 free).
+
+### DB2_GET_OBJECT_LIST
+
+`9f 02 00 00 00 ff*16` lists templates (type 2) with any parent: reply is
+a u16 count and that many 16 byte ids. `9f 03 00 00 00 <template id>`
+lists the payloads (type 3) below a template. The Windows driver does
+exactly this at open: DB info, template list, payload list per template.
+On this sensor 2 templates, 0 payloads below each. DB info says there are
+3 users and 4 payloads so users and payloads hang off something else.
+Type 1 was never seen on the wire and is not sent.
+
+## Pairing
+
+PAIR sends a host certificate and the sensor answers with the host
+certificate countersigned and its own certificate. The Windows driver does
+this on every open with a new key pair, the traces of two runs show two
+different host keys, and the saved Windows store only holds the latest
+one. Nothing is reused. The driver does the same: a fresh P-256 key
+per open, no persistent data. (libfprint has no per-device persistent data
+API anyway, only prints stored by the caller.) 25 opens in a row worked.
+
+### Certificate format (400 bytes)
+
+All integers little endian.
+
+| offset | size | field |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0 | 2 | magic 0x5f3f (bytes `3f 5f`) |
+| 2 | 2 | curve, 23 = secp256r1 |
+| 4 | 68 | x, 32 bytes used, zero padded |
+| 72 | 68 | y, same |
+| 140 | 1 | zero |
+| 141 | 1 | type (0 host request, 2 countersigned host, device cert is 0) |
+| 142 | 2 | signature size |
+| 144 | 256 | signature, zero padded |
+
+The signed part is the first 142 bytes. Host request: DER ECDSA SHA-256
+signature (71 bytes typically) by the "HS key". The countersigned host
+cert has a 32 byte signature (not checked, scheme unknown). The sensor
+cert has a DER ECDSA SHA-256 signature, checked against a per firmware
+public key.
+
+HS key: P-256 private key, derived as the TLS 1.2 PRF (SHA-256) with
+secret `717cd72d0962bc4a2846138dbb2c2419`, label `HS_KEY_PAIR_GEN`, seed
+`2512a76407065f383846139d4bec2033aaaa`, first 32 bytes read as a little
+endian integer. The constants come from the Windows driver. Upstream may
+not like shipping them, needs a decision.
+
+Sensor keys are known for firmware 10.1 with and without the key flag
+(public points are embedded in tudor_tls.c, from the prototype's .tsk
+files). This sensor has the key flag. Other firmware versions fail with
+NOT_SUPPORTED at pairing.
+
+## TLS 1.2
+
+Records travel inside TLS_DATA (0x44) during the handshake. After it,
+application data records are sent as the command itself, in place of the
+cleartext command, padded and with wValue as above. The reply is one
+record. Handshake exchange is:
+
+1. TLS_DATA with the ClientHello, reply is ServerHello + CertificateRequest
+   + ServerHelloDone in one record.
+2. TLS_DATA with Certificate + ClientKeyExchange + CertificateVerify
+   (one handshake record), ChangeCipherSpec and an encrypted Finished.
+   Reply is the sensor's ChangeCipherSpec and encrypted Finished.
+
+All the deviations from the standard:
+
+- ClientHello: version 3.3, 32 random bytes, 7 byte session id of zeros,
+  suites c005 c02e 003d 008d 00a8 00a9 (the sensor picks c02e,
+  ECDH_ECDSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384), **no compression methods** (length
+  byte 0), and the extensions follow **without an extensions length**:
+  supported_groups {secp256r1} and ec_point_formats {uncompressed}.
+- ServerHello: version field reads 0x0383, ignored. Session id is 7 bytes,
+  `54 4c 53` ("TLS") plus bytes 4..7 of the server random. No Certificate,
+  no ServerKeyExchange. CertificateRequest is `0d 000004 01 40 0000`.
+- Server key is the static key from the sensor certificate of the PAIR
+  reply. Premaster secret is the 32 byte x coordinate of the ECDH result
+  with an ephemeral client key.
+- Client Certificate message: `0b <len3> <400 as len3> <400 as len3> <2
+  junk bytes> <cert>`. The Windows driver leaves stack garbage in the junk
+  bytes, zeros work. The cert is the countersigned host cert from PAIR.
+- ClientKeyExchange is `04 x y` (65 bytes, big endian coordinates).
+- CertificateVerify is an ECDSA P-256 signature over the SHA-256 transcript
+  hash as is (prehashed). The sensor only accepts **exactly 70 bytes**:
+  `30 44 02 20 <r 32> 02 20 <s 32>` with no extra zero byte when the top
+  bit is set. DER from openssl is 71 or 72 bytes when r or s has the top
+  bit set (about half of the handshakes in testing) and those fail with alert 20 (bad
+  record mac) on the Finished. With the fixed layout 25 of 25 worked.
+- Transcript hash is SHA-256 over all handshake messages (4 byte header
+  included, as received/sent), the Finished messages are **not** part of
+  it, so client and server verify data use the same hash.
+- PRF is the TLS 1.2 PRF with HMAC-SHA-384. master secret = PRF(premaster,
+  "master secret", client_random + server_random, 48). Key block =
+  PRF(master, "key expansion", **client_random + server_random** (standard
+  is the other way round), 72). Client write key 32, server write key 32,
+  client salt 4, server salt 4.
+- Finished verify data = PRF(master, "client finished" or "server
+  finished", sha256 transcript, 12).
+- AES-256-GCM, explicit 8 byte random nonce in the record, nonce = salt +
+  explicit, AAD = seq (8, big endian) + type + 03 03 + plaintext length.
+  Sequence numbers count from 0 at the Finished.
+- Close: 0x1b, then 0x14 (still reads 1), then an encrypted close_notify
+  warning alert (`01 00`) as a command with wValue 7, the sensor answers
+  with its own close_notify.
+
+## Safety
+
+tudor_cmd_allowed() lets through only GET_VERSION, PAIR, TLS_DATA and the
+two DB reads. The driver never sends bootloader patch, peek/poke,
+provision, ownership commands, storage writes, DB writes, request 0x15, or
+anything else not seen in the traces.
+
+## Unknowns
+
+- Meaning of request 0x19/0x1a (a probe the Windows driver always does),
+  0x1b (session end).
+- Opcode 0xaf (`af 01 00 00 00`, 10 byte reply, after the status `0de2a15901ffffff`).
+- What the 2 templates and 4 payloads are in terms of fingers and users,
+  how a user object maps to a template, and what is in the 16 byte ids.
+  The 3 user objects are not listed by anything seen so far.
+- Payload object contents and the reply of OBJ_LIST for a template that
+  has payloads (only empty ones seen).
+- Status values other than 0.
+- The 32 byte signature scheme of the countersigned host cert, and whether
+  the sensor checks anything in the junk bytes of the Certificate message.
+- Whether other firmware versions use other TLS details. Only 10.1 tested.
+- Event endpoint 0x83 format (bytes 5 and 6 in the prototype notes are
+  frame index and event sequence).
+
+## What is needed for enroll, verify, identify
+
+Plaintext traces (same hook as for pair/reuse) with a finger on the
+sensor of: one enroll (all stages, including EVENT_CONFIG / EVENT_READ,
+LED_EX2, FRAME_ACQ/READ/FINISH, the DB2 object writes at the end and the
+reply of the final DB info and object list), one verify and one
+identify (IDENTIFY_WBF with its arguments and reply, and what happens on
+no match), and one delete of a single print. The two throwaway
+enrollments from capture-touch.sh would do.
